# binaries & build output
/curious
/bin/
/dist/

# Go workspace files (never committed)
go.work*

# GoReleaser
/.goreleaser/

# npm wrapper: what a local install or pack leaves behind. The package's
# own published file list is an allowlist, so none of this could ship —
# but the leak scan reads every untracked file no rule covers, and a
# downloaded binary is not something anybody authored.
/npm/node_modules/
/npm/*.tgz
/npm/installed.json
/npm/curious
/npm/curious.exe

# test & coverage
*.test
coverage.out
coverage.html

# local config used during manual testing — never commit a real token
.config/
*.token

# environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# orchestration half of CLAUDE.md — local only, never committed
CLAUDE.local.md

# Worktree lanes. A lane is a second checkout of THIS repository living
# inside it, and `git ls-files --others` reports it as one untracked
# directory — which internal/guard then walks, scanning another branch's
# files as though they were this checkout's. The visible symptom is the
# URL-constant ceiling counting every constant twice; the quieter one is
# that a guard's answer depends on which checkout you run it from, and
# the lane, where the work happens, is the one that cannot see it.
#
# Narrow on purpose: .claude/worktrees/ rather than .claude/, so anything
# this repository later decides to track under .claude/ is unaffected.
# Ignoring a lane is not a hole in the leak guard — a lane is a checkout
# of this repo, and its own CI scans it.
.claude/worktrees/
